<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.15.3: The Coordinates of the Vertex</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02012</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.15.3: The Coordinates of the Vertex</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b2c184d4-4005-4d1a-9885-effed5d0754e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <example id="exam-00001">
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph \(y=x^2\). Then, add different numbers to \(x\) before it is squared (for example, \(y=(x+4)^2\), \(y=(x−3)^2\)) and observe how the graph changes.</para>
            <para id="para-00002">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00004">Adding a number to \(x\) moves the graph to the left. Subtracting a number (or adding a negative number) moves the graph to the right. The vertex stays on the \(x\)-axis.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph \(y=(x−1)^2\). Then, experiment with each of the following changes to the function and see how they affect the graph and the vertex.</item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
        <item> Add different constant terms to \((x−1)^2\) (for example: \(y=(x−1)^2+5\), \(y=(x−1)^2−9)\). </item>
      </list>
      <div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
      <example id="exam-00002">
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00005">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <div class="os-raise-ib-content" data-schema-version="1.0" data-wait-for-event="Reveal2">
        <para id="para-00006">Compare your Answer:</para>
        <para id="para-00007">Adding a constant term moves it up and subtracting moves it down. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex doesn't change.</para>
      </div>
      <example id="exam-00003">
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00008"> Multiplying \((x−1)^2\) by different coefficients (for example: \(y=3(x−1)^2\), \(y=-2(x−1)^2\), \(y=0.5(x−1)^2\). </para>
            <para id="para-00009">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00010">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00011"> Multiplying the squared term by a number doesn't change the vertex, but it changes the opening of the graph to "wider" or "narrower," or changes the direction of the graph to open upward or downward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="f0b34626-53c2-444d-bdc8-ff755e0788a6" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00012">Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para id="para-00013">Without graphing, find the coordinates of the vertex of \(y=(x+10)^2\) and determine if it opens up or down.</para>
            <para id="para-00014"> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00015">Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is -10.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">The correct answer is is -10. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is -10.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="78115021-dd69-4349-a6b0-ee2dbb7a4169" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00017">Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para id="para-00018"> What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00019">Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is 0.</para>
            <para id="para-00020">The correct answer is is 0. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fa086711-1ff1-437c-a416-95dafb171f2b" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00021"> Does the graph open up or down? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00022">Correct! The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (1).</para>
            <para id="para-00023">Incorrect. The correct answer is Up. The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (1).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
