      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00007">
        <exercise id="06c86bbb-e9b6-4927-8751-95ce8e76aef4" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00027">
          <problem id="prob-00027">
            <para id="para-00094">Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para id="para-00095">Use the completed rows of the table to hypothesize about the coordinates of the vertex of the equation in the last row, and whether the parabola opens up or down.</para>
            <para id="para-00096"> For the equation \(y=a(x+m)^2+n\), what is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00026">
            <para id="para-00097">Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is –\(m\).</para>
            <para id="para-00098">The correct answer is is –\(m\). The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is –\(m\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="8e9f2598-2017-457c-bd50-9015f7ebfbb7" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00028">
          <problem id="prob-00028">
            <para id="para-00099">Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para id="para-00100"> For the equation \(y=a(x+m)^2+n\), what is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00027">
            <para id="para-00101">Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(n\).</para>
            <para id="para-00102">The correct answer is is \(n\). The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(n\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="10468be6-f473-420b-b5a3-b14329156584" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00029">
          <problem id="prob-00029">
            <para id="para-00103"> For the equation \(y=a(x+m)^2+n\), can you determine if the graph opens up or down with the information you are given? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00028">
            <para id="para-00104">Correct! No, because the direction of opening depends on whether \(a\) is positive or negative.</para>
            <para id="para-00105">Incorrect. No, because the direction of opening depends on whether \(a\) is positive or negative.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
